        <h1 class="title">Windoor Wizard Builder</h1>
        <p class="standfirst">
            A vertical SaaS with a fabrication engine that had to be exactly right.
        </p>

        <p class="lede">
            Egyptian UPVC window and door workshops run on WhatsApp, Excel, and paper. Quotes
            are priced by hand. Cut lists are worked out per job. The profile offcuts that
            decide whether a job is profitable are estimated rather than calculated.
        </p>

        <p>
            Generic ERP software does not help, because it does not model the domain. It has no
            concept of a frame deduction registry, of bar nesting, or of the glass area and
            steel reinforcement that drive the real cost of a window. A workshop using generic
            software still does the hard part on paper.
        </p>

        <h2>The decision that shaped everything</h2>

        <p>
            The single most consequential design decision in this system is how a length is
            stored. The obvious choice is <code>decimal</code> millimetres. It is wrong, and the
            reason is not theoretical.
        </p>

        <p class="pull">
            The shop cuts half-millimetres. In 195 verified rows from real orders,
            <strong>44 contained a half-millimetre cut</strong> — 483.5mm, not 483 and not 484.
            A cut list that rounds produces a window that does not fit, and the error is
            discovered after the profile has been cut.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
            <div class="code__head">
                <span class="mono">Millimetres.cs</span>
                <span class="mono code__tag">WWB.Fabrication</span>
            </div>
<pre class="mono"><span class="c">// There is deliberately NO decimal factory: a decimal entry point would invite
// rounding (Math.Round(483.5) == 484), destroying the very half-millimetre
// this type exists to preserve.</span>
<span class="k">public readonly record struct</span> <span class="t">Millimetres</span>
{
    <span class="k">public int</span> Tenths { <span class="k">get</span>; }

    <span class="k">public static</span> Millimetres <span class="f">FromTenths</span>(<span class="k">int</span> tenths) =&gt; <span class="k">new</span>(tenths);
    <span class="k">public static</span> Millimetres <span class="f">FromMm</span>(<span class="k">int</span> wholeMm)   =&gt; <span class="k">new</span>(wholeMm * <span class="n">10</span>);
}</pre>
        </div>

        <p>
            The part worth pausing on is the <em>absence</em> of a <code>FromDecimal</code>
            factory. Every length in the model either originates as whole millimetres — an
            opening measurement, a seed delta — or as the exact result of integer arithmetic on
            tenths. There is no legitimate path from a decimal into the model, and providing one
            would create an entry point through which rounding could reach the domain silently.
        </p>

        <p>
            This is a case where the right design was to make the wrong thing
            <strong>unrepresentable</strong> rather than to document it as discouraged. A comment
            saying "don't round here" is advice. A missing constructor is a guarantee.
        </p>

        <h2>Bar nesting: bin packing, with a saw</h2>

        <p>
            A window frame is cut from stock bars of fixed length, and profile is the dominant
            material cost. Deciding how to lay pieces onto bars is one-dimensional bin packing,
            which is NP-hard, so the engine uses First-Fit-Decreasing — a well-understood
            heuristic with a known worst-case bound. Three details separate the real
            implementation from the textbook one.
        </p>

        <dl class="points">
            <div>
                <dt>The saw has width</dt>
                <dd>
                    Every cut consumes kerf. A packer that ignores it produces arrangements that
                    fit on paper and fail on the machine. Kerf is charged per placement, not once
                    per bar, because it is consumed per cut.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>It fails loudly rather than degrading</dt>
                <dd>
                    A piece longer than a usable bar can never be cut, however the rest are
                    arranged. Returning a best-effort arrangement would hand the shop floor a cut
                    list that cannot be executed, so the operation fails and names the offending
                    measurement.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>Packing is deterministic</dt>
                <dd>
                    The sort is stable, so ties keep their input order and two runs over the same
                    order produce identical cut lists. A cut list is printed, taken to the machine,
                    and cross-checked against the order — a packing that reshuffled between runs
                    would make that check meaningless.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>Nothing is hardcoded</dt>
                <dd>
                    Bar length, end trim, saw kerf, and the minimum reusable offcut all arrive from
                    the caller. A different workshop with a different saw is a configuration change,
                    not a code change — the difference between software for one shop and a product.
                </dd>
            </div>
        </dl>

        <h2>Merging two systems into one bounded context</h2>

        <p>
            The two halves were built separately and each lacked what the other had. The SaaS
            shell had authentication, multi-tenancy, quotes, orders, and Arabic RTL PDF output —
            but its pricing engine was <strong>hollow</strong>; the quote calculation was stubbed.
            The fabrication engine <em>was</em> that missing calculation, verified against real
            orders — but it had no tenancy and no persistence beyond JSON files.
        </p>

        <figure class="arch">
            <div class="arch__row">
                <span class="box box--lead">WWB.Api</span>
            </div>
            <span class="arch__arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
            <div class="arch__row">
                <span class="box">WWB.Application &nbsp;·&nbsp; MediatR CQRS</span>
            </div>
            <div class="arch__row arch__row--three">
                <span class="box">WWB.Domain</span>
                <span class="box box--focus">WWB.Fabrication</span>
                <span class="box">WWB.Infrastructure</span>
            </div>
            <figcaption class="mono">tenant isolation via EF Core global query filters</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
            The engine became a <strong>bounded context inside the SaaS</strong> rather than a
            service alongside it. That choice is worth defending: the fabrication calculations
            always run in the context of a quote belonging to a tenant, they share that
            transaction, and they have no independent lifecycle. Splitting them across a network
            boundary would have bought distribution overhead and returned nothing.
        </p>

        <p>
            Multi-tenancy is enforced through EF Core global query filters on
            <code>TenantId</code>, not through a <code>WHERE</code> clause each query is trusted
            to remember. Tenant isolation implemented as a convention fails the first time
            somebody forgets; implemented as a query filter, forgetting is not possible. For a
            system holding one workshop's pricing against its competitors', that distinction is
            the whole security model.
        </p>

        <h2>Verified against reality, not against assumptions</h2>

        <p>
            The engine is <strong>oracle-verified</strong> — its output is checked against real
            orders from a working workshop, not against expectations derived from the same
            understanding that produced the code. A calculation engine validated only against its
            author's mental model is a mental model with tests. The merged solution carries
            <strong>491 passing unit tests</strong>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Alongside those, 72 tests are held deliberately red as the integration specification
            — and how that decision was reached is worth recording. After restructuring, those 72
            failed, all of them value assertions against the SaaS's stubbed pricing service. None
            were "type not found" errors, which is what a bad copy produces; the failures were
            pre-existing and <em>diagnostic</em>, specifying exactly which multipliers the real
            engine must satisfy once wired in. Deleting them would have made the suite green while
            destroying the only written record of that contract, so they were kept failing and
            documented as the acceptance criteria for integration.
        </p>

